{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfUTmFqhJes8ytX83VLiY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 05 is complete. Everything the item asked for is in place and verified.\n\n**What was built**\n- `learning/search_certificate.py` defines the certificate (one record per search node: move, free moves, kind, steps with rule and witness, children), an emitter that mirrors the reference Python search plus a Python port of the C's fixed Theorem 2 rule, and an independent checker that recomputes every premise from the matrix and the state and verifies exhaustiveness at each node.\n- `tests/test_search_certificate.py` adds 15 tests. The full suite passes: 1,153 passed, 2 skipped, 1 xfailed in 100 s.\n- Report section §32 in `reports/ml_nature.md`, tables in `reports/search_certificate_tables.md`, data in `learning/data/ensemble/search_certificate.csv`.\n\n**What was found**\n- Every one of the 6,135 certified corpus instances at 9–40 customers has a verified certificate under all three configurations, with zero rejections. This covers the 489 instances whose DRAT proof was censored in §17, and agrees with all 5,646 DRAT verdicts and the lattice oracle on 2,812.\n- The certificate tree is the C's tree without the memo, node for node: all 914 and 930 branch-count differences from the recorded C counts vanish when the C runs without its memo.\n- Certificates are about 60 bytes per branch and check at 15 µs per node. Against DRAT on the same instances they are 13× to 13,700× smaller and roughly 1,000× faster to check, with a different trust base, which the section states.\n- Both shipped bugs are reproduced and rejected for the right reason: the close-count bug fails a Theorem 2 premise, and the rule-order bug is rejected as a covering cycle with every premise true.\n- Kill verdict: a Theorem 2 step's premise is checkable from the instance and the state alone. What is not step-local is chain termination, a property of the node's whole step set. Theorem 3 is path-local, and the memo is checkable only without old move, which is exactly how the C differs from the certificate.\n\n**Loose ends noted in PROGRESS.md**\n- At 41–75 customers, 141 of 151 instances are certified under both configurations. Ten are censored at 60 s, all at 75 customers, because the emitter is Python.\n- No `benchmarks.recertify` Python process was actually running; the pgrep pattern matches the session's own prompt text.\n- Nothing was committed and nothing was written to `solutions/`. Eight items remain, so no LOOP_COMPLETE line was added."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3144,"cache_read_input_tokens":201124,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3144,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b5a4109d-e2ad-4e5e-b1e8-33d870a2ac2e","uuid":"be991df1-f3cf-453b-afeb-e12bdcb4728b","timestamp":"2026-09-27T19:02:41.093Z","request_id":"req_011CfUTmEij34TVc4p3iPPD4"}
